IMPORT prelude

-- Rates and Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Act 1992 (Western Australia), with the Rates and Charges (Rebates and
-- Deferments) Regulations 1992 -- the pensioner or senior ratepayer: am I entitled to a rebate on my council rates, the
-- emergency services levy and my water, sewerage and drainage charges, how much, by when must I pay the rest, may I
-- defer it, and when do deferred charges become payable.
--
-- SEAT: the pensioner or senior ratepayer -- the individual who owns (or holds a relevant interest in) the home on which
-- a prescribed charge is made ("Am I entitled to a rates rebate or deferment?"). Every exported question is phrased as
-- that person would ask it. The administrative authority (the local government, the Water Corporation), the Minister and
-- the CEO who issues State concession cards appear only as what they mean for the ratepayer: whether the entitlement
-- must be registered, what rebate must be allowed, what may be declined, and when deferred charges can be recovered.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 3(1) definitions ("prescribed charge", "eligible pensioner", "eligible senior", "eligible person",
-- "rebate", "rebated amount", "final payment day", "rating year", "spouse", "de facto partner", "pensioner concession
-- card", "Commonwealth seniors health card", "seniors' card", "State concession card"); s 5 (seniors' card conditions
-- set by the CEO: a FACT); s 22 (seniors); s 23(1), (1a), (2), (3), (4A), (4), (5) (pensioners: the concession card and
-- the means test; veterans; a seniors' card with a Commonwealth seniors health card; relatives of a disabled occupant);
-- s 24 (other prescribed eligibility); s 25 (prescribed means tests: none is prescribed in the deposited Regulations);
-- s 27(1)-(2), (5) (land belonging to a person; a resident spouse's share counted as one's own); s 28(1)(b), (2), (3)
-- (apportionment; at most 50%; election of one basis); ss 29, 29A, 29B, 29C (relevant interests, as one choice);
-- s 30(1) (ordinary place of residence while away); s 32(1a), (3), (4) (registration; one place; not both bases);
-- s 33(2), (3), (6), (7) (effect of registration; death or change mid-year; arrears); s 35(1), (4) (changes;
-- the means test exceeded); s 38 (offences, $1 000; 5 years); s 40 (the rebate: (1), (2), (2A), (3A), (5)-(7), (9));
-- s 41 (paying the rebated amount ends liability); s 43 (when deferment may be allowed); s 44(1), (2), (2A) (deferment
-- of an unpaid rebated amount; when deferred charges become payable); s 45 (deferment continued for a spouse or de
-- facto partner); s 46(1) (a charge on the land); s 47 (deferment declined where not recoverable: answered
-- conditionally); s 49 (the Limitation Act 2005 does not bar recovery). Regulations r 3(2) (seniors' limits) and
-- r 4(1A), (2) (pensioners' limits), for rating years beginning on or after 1 July 2018.
--
-- Edition: compilation of the Rates and Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Act 1992 (No. 31 of 1992) as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_47938.txt (legislation.wa.gov.au mrdoc 47938, retrieved 2026-09-07), last amended
-- in the deposit by No. 42 of 2024 s 58 (s 29A). Rates and Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Regulations 1992, version
-- 04-h0-02 (currency from 13 Jun 2018), as deposited at
-- ../../../rates-and-charges-rebates-and-deferments-regulations-1992/registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_40959.txt (mrdoc
-- 40959, retrieved 2026-10-20), last amended by the Amendment Regulations 2018 (Gazette 12 Jun 2018).
--
-- THE EQUATIONS. s 40(5), (6) and (7) each say "the rebate to be allowed shall be determined using the following
-- equation". The equations are images that did not survive into the deposited text; they are transcribed by hand from
-- the authorised Word rendering of the same document (mrdoc 47938) in
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_47938.formulas.md (2026-10-21):
--   s 40(5)  R = PC x (DS / CY)
--   s 40(6)  R = PC x (DP / CY)
--   s 40(7)  R = (PCS x (DS / CY)) + (PCP x (DP / CY))
--
-- NOT ENCODED: Part 1 Division 4 and Part 1A (purposes, ministerial directions, procedural manuals, delegation, reviews
-- by an authorised review officer, compensation for errors, claims for reimbursement: ss 8-17); transitional Part 1B
-- (ss 18-21) and Schedule 1; s 6 (how the CEO issues a State concession card: holding one is a FACT); s 26 (evidence:
-- holding a card is taken as shown); s 28(1)(a), (4), (5) and s 27(3)-(5a) beyond the share input; the detail of each
-- relevant interest in ss 29-29C (one choice of interest is asked); s 30(2); s 31 (former joint occupation and
-- separation) except as s 44(2)(c) uses it; s 32A (surviving partner's registration); ss 36, 37 (review, amendment and
-- cancellation by the authority); s 39 (rebates allowed incorrectly); s 40(3) (discounts), (8) (refund of excess);
-- s 42 (back-dated charges and charges for improvements); s 44(3), s 46(2)-(3) (priority of housing debts); s 48;
-- the rating years before 1 July 2018 in the Regulations' tables; soil and land conservation rates and any charge
-- prescribed under paragraph (d) of "prescribed charge"; consumption-based water charges (outside "prescribed charge").
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the seat of the pensioner or senior ratepayer, with the
-- design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for
-- public use as advice. See NOTES.md.
--
-- Conventions. A rating year runs 1 July to 30 June (s 3(1): "rating year means 1 July to 30 June"), named by the
-- calendar year in which it ENDS: 2073/74 is 2074. A day is written as the number YYYYMMDD. Money is in dollars.
-- A share is a fraction between 0 and 1.

§ `Rates and Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Act 1992 -- the pensioner or senior ratepayer`

§§ `Arithmetic`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

§§ `ss 22, 23, 24, 25 -- am I an eligible pensioner or an eligible senior?`

-- s 3(1): "eligible pensioner means a person to whom section 23 or section 24 applies"; "eligible senior means a person to
-- whom section 22 applies"; "eligible person means an eligible pensioner or an eligible senior".
-- "pensioner concession card means (a) a currently valid pensioner concession card issued by or on behalf of the
-- Commonwealth Government; and (b) a card prescribed by the regulations ..., but does not include a pensioner concession
-- card ... excluded from this definition under the regulations". (The deposited Regulations prescribe and exclude none.)
-- "Commonwealth seniors health card means a currently valid card, known by that name, issued on behalf of the
-- Commonwealth". "seniors' card means a card (a) which bears that designation; and (b) is issued to the holder by the
-- senior's card department". s 5: the conditions for holding a seniors' card "shall be such as are from time to time
-- determined by the chief executive officer of the senior's card department" -- so the Act sets no age: holding the card
-- is a FACT.
-- "pension means test" is the Social Security Act 1991 (Cth) Chapter 3 ordinary income, maintenance income and assets
-- tests, unless another test is prescribed under s 25. The deposited Regulations prescribe none: whether I am within
-- the test is a FACT.
DECLARE `My cards and circumstances` HAS
    `I hold a pensioner concession card` IS A BOOLEAN
    `my card is of a class the regulations exclude` IS A BOOLEAN
    `my income and assets are within the pension means test` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am permanently blind` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am a veteran, war widow or dependant within section 23(2), (3) or (4A)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I hold a seniors card` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I hold a Commonwealth seniors health card` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I hold a State concession card` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I have an eligibility prescribed for section 24` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am related to a disabled person who lives on the land and no owner lives there` IS A BOOLEAN

-- s 23(1): eligible "so long as (a) that person is the holder of a pensioner concession card; and ... (c) unless the person
-- is permanently blind, the person does not possess income or assets of a value in excess of that permitted by any
-- prescribed means test applicable." (1a): "The regulations may provide that a person of a prescribed class is not
-- eligible ... despite being the holder of a pensioner concession card."
-- s 23(2), (3), (4A): certain veterans under the Veterans' Entitlements Act 1986, war widows and widowed or unmarried
-- mothers of deceased members of the Forces, and persons under the Military Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 2004
-- (borrowed Commonwealth terms: one FACT), "regardless of the income or assets of that person" ((2), (4A)).
-- s 23(4): "a person who is the holder of both a seniors' card and a Commonwealth seniors health card is eligible".
-- s 23(5): a person "related to a disabled person who occupies the land as his or her ordinary place of residence" where
-- "no owner of the land occupies the land" (parents, grandparents, brothers and sisters of a person on the disability
-- support pension, or of a child under 16 whose carer receives carer payment: s 23(6)-(7)).
-- s 24: "(a) the person (i) possesses an eligibility of a kind prescribed for the purposes of this section; and (ii) is the
-- holder of a pensioner concession card or a State concession card; and (b) unless the person is permanently blind," is
-- within the means test. (The deposited Regulations prescribe no eligibility for s 24.)
GIVEN `me` IS A `My cards and circumstances`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`I pass the means test or am blind` MEANS
    `me`'s `I am permanently blind` OR `me`'s `my income and assets are within the pension means test`

@export Am I an eligible pensioner under any limb other than section 23(5) (ss 23(1)-(4), 24)?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My cards and circumstances` @desc Your concession cards, pension and means-test position
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I an eligible pensioner in my own right` IF
        (    `me`'s `I hold a pensioner concession card`
         AND (NOT (`me`'s `my card is of a class the regulations exclude`))
         AND `I pass the means test or am blind` `me`)
    OR `me`'s `I am a veteran, war widow or dependant within section 23(2), (3) or (4A)`
    OR (`me`'s `I hold a seniors card` AND `me`'s `I hold a Commonwealth seniors health card`)
    OR (    `me`'s `I have an eligibility prescribed for section 24`
        AND (`me`'s `I hold a pensioner concession card` OR `me`'s `I hold a State concession card`)
        AND `I pass the means test or am blind` `me`)

@export Am I an eligible pensioner (ss 3(1), 23, 24)?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My cards and circumstances` @desc Your concession cards, pension and means-test position
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I an eligible pensioner` IF
        `Am I an eligible pensioner in my own right` `me`
    OR `me`'s `I am related to a disabled person who lives on the land and no owner lives there`

-- s 22: "If, under section 40, a rebate is to be allowable to seniors on any prescribed charge, a person who holds a
-- seniors' card is ... eligible to apply". s 40(9)(a), (c) do allow seniors a rebate.
@export Am I an eligible senior (ss 3(1), 22)?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My cards and circumstances` @desc Your concession cards
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I an eligible senior` IF `me`'s `I hold a seniors card`

-- s 28(3): "Notwithstanding that a person may be both an eligible pensioner and an eligible senior that person, pursuant
-- to section 32(4), must elect under which basis of eligibility the entitlement of that person is to be registered".
-- s 32(4)(b): not registered "both as an eligible senior and as an eligible pensioner". The pensioner basis gives 50%
-- against the senior's 25% (s 40(9)) and alone allows deferment (s 43(1)(a)); so it is the better election
-- (INFERENCE: the Act leaves the choice to the person).
DECLARE `My basis of eligibility` IS ONE OF
    `as an eligible pensioner`
    `as an eligible senior`
    `not eligible`

@export On which basis should I register: as an eligible pensioner or as an eligible senior (ss 28(3), 32(4))?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My cards and circumstances` @desc Your concession cards, pension and means-test position
GIVETH A `My basis of eligibility`
DECIDE `On which basis should I register` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `Am I an eligible pensioner` `me` THEN `as an eligible pensioner`
        IF `Am I an eligible senior` `me` THEN `as an eligible senior`
        OTHERWISE `not eligible`

§§ `ss 27, 29-29C, 30, 32 -- does my home count, and must my entitlement be registered?`

-- s 27(1): "If a person holds an estate in fee simple in possession in land, a strata lease as defined in the Strata Titles
-- Act 1985 or a relevant interest in land, the land shall ... be treated as belonging to that person". Relevant interests
-- (ss 29-29C): a Crown lease or licence, a beneficiary or life tenant in a deceased estate, a protective deed or trust,
-- company title, a purchaser in possession under a contract (s 29); a retirement village resident (s 29A), the owner-
-- occupier of a caravan or park home site (s 29B) or of a relocatable home site (s 29C), each paying the charge under a
-- written "prescribed charge arrangement". A tenant holds none of these (INFERENCE from the list).
DECLARE `My interest in my home` IS ONE OF
    `fee simple or strata lease`
    `retirement village residence with a prescribed charge arrangement`
    `caravan or park home site with a prescribed charge arrangement`
    `relocatable home site with a prescribed charge arrangement`
    `another relevant interest under section 29`
    `no estate or relevant interest`

-- s 27(5): where the land is "the ordinary place of residence in which both that person and the spouse or de facto
-- partner of that person live together, any entitlement ... held by the spouse or de facto partner shall be taken into
-- account as if it were the entitlement of that person". "spouse" and "de facto partner" exclude one living "separately
-- and apart" on "an apparently permanent and voluntary basis". So `my share of the land` counts a resident partner's
-- share as mine.
DECLARE `My home` HAS
    `my interest` IS A `My interest in my home`
    `I live there as my ordinary place of residence` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I left because of ill-health, frailty or another cause beyond my control` IS A BOOLEAN
    `my furniture or personal effects remain there` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is empty, or lived in by my dependant, or rent-free by another with my consent` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I have another entitlement registered` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am the sole owner` IS A BOOLEAN
    `my only co-owner is my spouse or de facto partner` IS A BOOLEAN
    `my share of the land` IS A NUMBER
    `the share of the land used as my residence` IS A NUMBER

@export Is my home treated as belonging to me (ss 27(1), 29-29C)?
GIVEN `the home` IS A `My home` @desc Your home and your interest in it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my home belong to me` IS
    CONSIDER `the home`'s `my interest`
    WHEN `no estate or relevant interest` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 30(1): land "shall be deemed to be the ordinary place of residence of that person if (a) the land belongs to that
-- person, and was formerly the ordinary place of residence of that person; and (b) furniture, household goods or personal
-- effects of that person remain in the residence; and (c) the land is (i) unoccupied; or (ii) occupied by any dependant
-- ...; or (iii) with the consent of that person occupied by some other person otherwise than on a paying basis; and (d)
-- that person has not for the time being any other entitlement registered, where the residence of that person there
-- ceased by reason of ill-health, frailty or other cause not within the control of that person."
@export Is my home still my ordinary place of residence, even while I am away from it (s 30(1))?
GIVEN `the home` IS A `My home` @desc Your home and whether you still live there
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my home my ordinary place of residence` IF
        `the home`'s `I live there as my ordinary place of residence`
    OR (    `Does my home belong to me` `the home`
        AND `the home`'s `I left because of ill-health, frailty or another cause beyond my control`
        AND `the home`'s `my furniture or personal effects remain there`
        AND `the home`'s `it is empty, or lived in by my dependant, or rent-free by another with my consent`
        AND (NOT (`the home`'s `I have another entitlement registered`)))

-- s 27(2): "Where land belongs to an applicant who (a) occupies the land, or a part of it, as his or her ordinary place of
-- residence; and (b) is an eligible person, the extent of the entitlement of the applicant as regards the land shall,
-- subject to this Act, be registered." s 32(3): registration is required "even if arrears in respect of a prescribed
-- charge in relation to that land remain payable". s 32(4)(a): not "for the same person, in respect of more than one place
-- of residence" (except on the s 23(5) basis: s 32(4a)). For s 23(5) the person need not own or live there: the disabled
-- relative does (s 23(5)(a)); s 30(2) takes the land to be wholly occupied by the registered person.
@export Must my entitlement to this home be registered (ss 23(5), 27(2), 32(3)-(4a))?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My cards and circumstances` @desc Your concession cards, pension and means-test position
      `the home` IS A `My home` @desc Your home and your interest in it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my entitlement be registered` IF
        (    `Does my home belong to me` `the home`
         AND `Is my home my ordinary place of residence` `the home`
         AND (`Am I an eligible pensioner in my own right` `me` OR `Am I an eligible senior` `me`)
         AND (NOT (`the home`'s `I have another entitlement registered`)))
    OR `me`'s `I am related to a disabled person who lives on the land and no owner lives there`

-- s 32(1a): "An application ... to a local government, in relation to a prescribed charge by way of rates, is to be taken
-- to include an application ... in relation to the emergency services levy."
@export Does my application to the council for a rates rebate also count for the emergency services levy (s 32(1a))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my rates application also cover the emergency services levy` IS TRUE

§§ `s 33 -- arrears, and a change during the year`

-- s 33(3): "Where arrears are due from a registered person in respect of a prescribed charge neither payment of a rebated
-- amount nor deferment ... shall be allowed ..., unless subsection (4) or (6) applies." (6): an eligible pensioner may pay
-- a rebated amount or defer "if that person and the administrative authority have agreed on an arrangement for the
-- payment of arrears". (7): if the arrears due in the charged period are not paid under the arrangement, the authority
-- "may by written notice" require the full charge and the person "ceases to be entitled". (Subsection (4), arrangements
-- made before 1993, is not encoded.)
@export With arrears outstanding, may I still pay only the rebated amount, or defer (s 33(3), (6), (7))?
GIVEN `my basis` IS A `My basis of eligibility` @desc The basis on which you are registered
      `I owe arrears of this charge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether arrears of this kind of charge are due from you
      `I have agreed an arrears arrangement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the council or Water Corporation agreed an arrangement to pay the arrears
      `I have been given notice for breaking the arrangement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you missed the arrangement's payments and were given written notice to pay the full charge
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I pay only the rebated amount despite arrears` IS
    IF `I owe arrears of this charge`
    THEN CONSIDER `my basis`
         WHEN `as an eligible pensioner` THEN
             `I have agreed an arrears arrangement` AND (NOT `I have been given notice for breaking the arrangement`)
         OTHERWISE FALSE
    ELSE TRUE

-- The link the Local Government Act 1995 s 6.51(4) and the Fire and Emergency Services Act 1998 s 36S ask about: a
-- person "entitled under the Rates and Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Act 1992 ... to a rebate or deferment". Here:
-- an entitlement that must be registered, on which the rebate or deferment is not barred by arrears.
@export Am I entitled to a rebate or deferment of the rates on my home under this Act (ss 27(2), 33(3), 40(1))?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My cards and circumstances` @desc Your concession cards, pension and means-test position
      `the home` IS A `My home` @desc Your home and your interest in it
      `I owe arrears of this charge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether arrears of this kind of charge are due from you
      `I have agreed an arrears arrangement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you agreed an arrangement to pay the arrears
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I entitled to a rebate or deferment` IF
        `Must my entitlement be registered` `me` `the home`
    AND `May I pay only the rebated amount despite arrears` (`On which basis should I register` `me`)
            `I owe arrears of this charge` `I have agreed an arrears arrangement` FALSE

-- s 33(2): for a charge that is not a Water Corporation charge, "effect shall be given ... to any rebate or deferment
-- allowable for that charged period ... notwithstanding that during the charged period (a) the registered person dies, or
-- ceases to be an eligible person; or (b) the land ceases to belong to the registered person, but effect shall not be
-- given to the rebate or deferment in relation to any subsequent charged period." For a Water Corporation charge a
-- cancellation runs from "the day on which the change in the person's circumstances occurred" (s 37(1)(c)).
@export If I die, stop being eligible, or the home stops being mine, does my rebate still apply for that charged period (s 33(2))?
GIVEN `it is a Water Corporation charge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the charge is made by the Water Corporation
      `the charged period is the one in which the change happened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you ask about the charged period in which the change happened, rather than a later one
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my rebate still apply for the charged period of the change` IF
        (NOT `it is a Water Corporation charge`)
    AND `the charged period is the one in which the change happened`

§§ `s 40 and the Regulations -- how much is my rebate?`

-- s 3(1) "prescribed charge": (a) rates under the Local Government Act 1995 (or the Soil and Land Conservation Act 1945);
-- (b) a charge for water supply, sewerage or drainage "not being a charge assessed by reference to the quantity of water
-- or wastewater concerned"; (c) the emergency services levy; (da) a service charge under the Local Government Act 1995
-- s 6.38 for underground electricity.
DECLARE `Kind of charge` IS ONE OF
    `council rates`
    `emergency services levy`
    `water supply charge`
    `sewerage charge`
    `drainage charge`
    `underground electricity service charge`

-- s 40(9): "(a) for an eligible senior, on any prescribed charge by way of the emergency services levy or rates or for the
-- provision of water supply, sewerage or drainage -- 25% of the prescribed charge concerned, up to such limit as is
-- prescribed; (b) for an eligible pensioner, on any prescribed charge by way of the emergency services levy or rates or
-- for the provision of water supply, sewerage or drainage or underground electricity -- 50% ..., up to such limit as is
-- prescribed; (c) for an eligible senior, on any prescribed charge for the provision of underground electricity -- 25%".
@export What percentage of the charge is my rebate before any limit (s 40(9))?
GIVEN `my basis` IS A `My basis of eligibility` @desc The basis on which you are registered
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What fraction of the charge is my rebate` IS
    CONSIDER `my basis`
    WHEN `as an eligible pensioner` THEN 0.5
    WHEN `as an eligible senior` THEN 0.25
    WHEN `not eligible` THEN 0

-- The prescribed limits, for a rating year beginning on or after 1 July 2018.
-- Regulations r 4(1A): pensioners -- "(a) ... (i) water supply -- $108.86; (ii) sewerage -- $436.15; (iii) drainage --
-- $54.99; (b) a charge, by way of rates, made under the Local Government Act 1995 -- $750.00." r 4(2): "A limit is not
-- placed on the amount of rebate that a pensioner is to be allowed on ... (b) a charge by way of the emergency services
-- levy; or (c) [the underground electricity service charge]".
-- Regulations r 3(2): seniors -- "(a) ... (i) water supply -- $18.14; (ii) sewerage -- $72.69; (iii) drainage -- $9.17;
-- (b) a charge, by way of rates, made under the Local Government Act 1995 -- $100.00; (c) a charge for the provision of
-- underground electricity -- $100.00." r 3(2) prescribes no limit for the emergency services levy, so a senior's ESL
-- rebate is 25% without a limit (INFERENCE: s 40(9)(a) caps it only "up to such limit as is prescribed").
-- A charge with no limit is given the limit -1 here, read as "no limit".
@export What is the most rebate the Regulations allow me on this kind of charge, or -1 if there is no limit (Regulations rr 3(2), 4(1A), 4(2))?
GIVEN `my basis` IS A `My basis of eligibility` @desc The basis on which you are registered
      `the kind` IS A `Kind of charge` @desc The kind of prescribed charge
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the prescribed limit on my rebate` IS
    CONSIDER `my basis`
    WHEN `as an eligible pensioner` THEN
        CONSIDER `the kind`
        WHEN `council rates` THEN 750
        WHEN `emergency services levy` THEN -1
        WHEN `water supply charge` THEN 108.86
        WHEN `sewerage charge` THEN 436.15
        WHEN `drainage charge` THEN 54.99
        WHEN `underground electricity service charge` THEN -1
    WHEN `as an eligible senior` THEN
        CONSIDER `the kind`
        WHEN `council rates` THEN 100
        WHEN `emergency services levy` THEN -1
        WHEN `water supply charge` THEN 18.14
        WHEN `sewerage charge` THEN 72.69
        WHEN `drainage charge` THEN 9.17
        WHEN `underground electricity service charge` THEN 100
    WHEN `not eligible` THEN 0

-- s 28(1)(b): the authority "shall apportion any prescribed charge, and any rebate allowable, according to (i) the extent
-- of the several respective interests ...; and (ii) if the applicant ... does not occupy the whole of the land as their
-- ordinary place of residence, the extent to which it is so occupied". s 28(2): likewise where residence "is not the
-- sole use of that land". s 40(1A): my liability is "the proportion of the prescribed charge that relates to the extent
-- of the entitlement of the person".
@export What part of the charge is mine, after apportionment for shared ownership and other uses (ss 28(1)(b), 28(2), 40(1A))?
GIVEN `the home` IS A `My home` @desc Your home: your share and the share you live in
      `the charge` IS A NUMBER @desc The whole prescribed charge on the land for the charged period
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What part of the charge is mine` IS
    `the charge` TIMES `the home`'s `my share of the land` TIMES `the home`'s `the share of the land used as my residence`

-- s 40(9): PC, "the portion of the prescribed charge calculated under subsection (9)", is the percentage of my part of
-- the charge, held to the prescribed limit. Applying the limit after apportionment (not before) is an INFERENCE: s 28
-- apportions "any rebate allowable" and the Act does not say in which order.
GIVEN `my basis` IS A `My basis of eligibility`
      `the kind` IS A `Kind of charge`
      `my part` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the portion for a full year` MEANS
    IF `What is the prescribed limit on my rebate` `my basis` `the kind` LESS THAN 0
    THEN `What fraction of the charge is my rebate` `my basis` TIMES `my part`
    ELSE `the lesser of` (`What fraction of the charge is my rebate` `my basis` TIMES `my part`)
                         (`What is the prescribed limit on my rebate` `my basis` `the kind`)

-- s 40(5)-(7): R from PC (or PCS and PCP), "DS ... the number of the days during the charged period that the person is
-- registered as an eligible senior", "DP ... as an eligible pensioner", and "CY ... the number of days in the charged
-- period". s 40(7) as printed (mrdoc_47938.formulas.md): R = (PCS x (DS / CY)) + (PCP x (DP / CY)). It reduces to
-- s 40(5), R = PC x (DS / CY), when DP is 0 and to s 40(6), R = PC x (DP / CY), when DS is 0, so one rule serves all three.
-- s 28(3): where the land belongs to me and my partner or to several persons, "the maximum rebate ... shall not exceed
-- 50%" -- never exceeded here, since no percentage in s 40(9)(a)-(c) is above 50%.
@export How much is my rebate on this charge for the charged period (ss 28, 40(4)-(7), (9); Regulations rr 3, 4)?
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of charge` @desc The kind of prescribed charge
      `the home` IS A `My home` @desc Your home: your share and the share you live in
      `the charge` IS A NUMBER @desc The whole prescribed charge on the land for the charged period
      `days registered as a pensioner` IS A NUMBER @desc Days of the charged period you were registered as an eligible pensioner
      `days registered as a senior` IS A NUMBER @desc Days of the charged period you were registered as an eligible senior
      `days in the charged period` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days in the charged period
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much is my rebate` IS
    (`the portion for a full year` `as an eligible senior` `the kind` (`What part of the charge is mine` `the home` `the charge`)
        TIMES (`days registered as a senior` DIVIDED BY `days in the charged period`))
    PLUS
    (`the portion for a full year` `as an eligible pensioner` `the kind` (`What part of the charge is mine` `the home` `the charge`)
        TIMES (`days registered as a pensioner` DIVIDED BY `days in the charged period`))

-- s 3(1): "rebated amount means the balance, after the rebate is allowed, of a prescribed charge payable by a person who
-- has a registered entitlement".
@export How much must I pay after my rebate: the rebated amount (ss 3(1), 40(1A), 40(2))?
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of charge` @desc The kind of prescribed charge
      `the home` IS A `My home` @desc Your home: your share and the share you live in
      `the charge` IS A NUMBER @desc The whole prescribed charge on the land for the charged period
      `days registered as a pensioner` IS A NUMBER @desc Days of the charged period you were registered as an eligible pensioner
      `days registered as a senior` IS A NUMBER @desc Days of the charged period you were registered as an eligible senior
      `days in the charged period` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days in the charged period
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is my rebated amount` IS
    `What part of the charge is mine` `the home` `the charge`
    MINUS `How much is my rebate` `the kind` `the home` `the charge`
              `days registered as a pensioner` `days registered as a senior` `days in the charged period`

-- s 40(2A): "this section does not apply to an eligible senior in respect of a prescribed charge for the provision of
-- underground electricity for any charged period other than the first year or part of the first year for which that
-- charge is to be or was made."
@export As a senior, do I get a rebate on the underground electricity service charge this year (s 40(2A))?
GIVEN `my basis` IS A `My basis of eligibility` @desc The basis on which you are registered
      `this is the first year of the charge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this is the first year, or part year, for which the underground electricity charge is made
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I get a rebate on the underground electricity charge` IS
    CONSIDER `my basis`
    WHEN `as an eligible pensioner` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `as an eligible senior` THEN `this is the first year of the charge`
    WHEN `not eligible` THEN FALSE

§§ `ss 40(2), (3A), 41 -- by when must I pay the rebated amount?`

-- s 40(2): the liability "may ... be satisfied by the payment of a rebated amount (a) in the case of a Water Corporation
-- charge -- by the final payment day or, if that person has been allowed an extended period under subsection (3A), before
-- the end of that period; or (b) in the case of any other prescribed charge -- before the end of the charged period or
-- [the extended period]". (3A): the Minister may allow an extended period if "satisfied that a person ... has, because of
-- exceptional circumstances, failed to pay" (answered conditionally, as an input).
-- For council rates and the ESL the charged period is the rating year, ending 30 June (s 3(1) "charged period", "year
-- means a rating year"; a part-year charge is not modelled).
@export By what day must I pay the rebated amount of my council rates or emergency services levy (s 40(2)(b), (3A))?
GIVEN `the rating year` IS A NUMBER @desc The rating year, named by the year in which it ends (2073/74 is 2074)
      `the Minister allowed me an extended period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Minister, satisfied of exceptional circumstances, allowed you an extended period
      `the end of the extended period` IS A NUMBER @desc The last day of that extended period, as YYYYMMDD (ignored if none)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `By what day must I pay my rebated rates` IS
    IF `the Minister allowed me an extended period`
    THEN `the end of the extended period`
    ELSE `the rating year` TIMES 10000 PLUS 630

-- s 3(1): "final payment day for a Water Corporation charge ... means (a) the 50th day after the date of the notice
-- requiring payment for that charge; or (b) a day, determined by the Water Corporation in relation to the person, that is
-- after the day referred to in paragraph (a) but not more than 70 days after that day".
@export How many days after my Water Corporation notice is my final payment day for the rebated amount (s 3(1), s 40(2)(a))?
GIVEN `extra days the Water Corporation allowed me` IS A NUMBER @desc Further days beyond the 50th day that the Water Corporation determined for you (0 if none)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days after my water notice is my final payment day` IS
    50 PLUS `the lesser of` `extra days the Water Corporation allowed me` 70

-- s 41(2): if the rebated amount of a charge that is not a Water Corporation charge "is paid ... before the end of the
-- charged period" (or extended period), "(a) that person shall not be liable to pay any further amount for that
-- prescribed charge". s 41(3): the same for a Water Corporation charge paid by the final payment day.
-- s 44(1): an eligible pensioner who "does not pay, or does not wholly pay, the rebated amount ... by the day ... (a)
-- remains liable for the payment of the full amount of the charge, without rebate; and (b) ceases to be authorised to
-- satisfy the liability ... by the payment only of a rebated amount, but the administrative authority shall not seek to
-- recover the amount ... and the liability ... may be deferred". A senior has no s 44: missing the day leaves the full
-- charge owing (INFERENCE from s 40(2): the rebated amount satisfies the charge only if paid in time).
DECLARE `What paying or not paying leads to` IS ONE OF
    `nothing more is owed for that charge`
    `the full charge is owed, but deferred and not pursued`
    `the full charge is owed without rebate`

@export What happens if I pay, or do not pay, the rebated amount by the day (ss 40(2), 41, 44(1))?
GIVEN `my basis` IS A `My basis of eligibility` @desc The basis on which you are registered
      `I paid the rebated amount in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid the whole rebated amount by the day
      `I may defer this charge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether deferment is open to you under section 43 (see that question)
GIVETH A `What paying or not paying leads to`
DECIDE `What happens to the charge` IS
    IF `I paid the rebated amount in time`
    THEN `nothing more is owed for that charge`
    ELSE CONSIDER `my basis`
         WHEN `as an eligible pensioner` THEN
             IF `I may defer this charge`
             THEN `the full charge is owed, but deferred and not pursued`
             ELSE `the full charge is owed without rebate`
         OTHERWISE `the full charge is owed without rebate`

§§ `ss 43, 47 -- may I defer paying?`

-- s 43(1): deferment may be allowed where "(a) the person liable ... is an eligible pensioner ...; and (b) the conditions
-- to which a rebate of that charge is subject have been met; and (c) the land ... belongs to one person only, unless
-- subsection (3) or subsection (4) applies, but the administrative authority under section 47 may decline". (1a): not for
-- a relevant interest under s 29A, 29B or 29C. (1b): not if "registered on the basis of eligibility under section
-- 23(5)". (2): only "as the sole owner of the fee simple in possession ... or as the sole owner of a lot in a leasehold
-- scheme" or under (3) or (4). (3): tenants in common, where the charge is assessed "only of the specific part of that
-- land which (a) is in the exclusive occupation of; and (b) is the ordinary place of residence of" the person. (4): a
-- co-registered "other person" who "(a) is the spouse or de facto partner of the person liable; or (b) ... occupies the
-- land as his or her ordinary place of residence; and ... is also eligible to defer, and agrees". s 47: where "the likely
-- net sale value of the land would be insufficient", the authority "may decline to permit deferral" (an input).
-- Seniors cannot defer: s 43(1)(a) speaks only of an eligible pensioner.
DECLARE `My deferment circumstances` HAS
    `the charge is assessed only on the part I alone occupy as a tenant in common` IS A BOOLEAN
    `my co-owner lives there, may also defer, and agrees` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the authority declined because the land would not cover the charges` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the home` IS A `My home`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`I hold the fee simple or a strata lease` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the home`'s `my interest`
    WHEN `fee simple or strata lease` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export May I defer paying my rates and charges (ss 43, 47)?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My cards and circumstances` @desc Your concession cards, pension and means-test position
      `the home` IS A `My home` @desc Your home and your interest in it
      `the circumstances` IS A `My deferment circumstances` @desc Co-ownership and the authority's view of recoverability
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I defer payment` IF
        `Am I an eligible pensioner in my own right` `me`
    AND (NOT (`me`'s `I am related to a disabled person who lives on the land and no owner lives there`))
    AND `Must my entitlement be registered` `me` `the home`
    AND `I hold the fee simple or a strata lease` `the home`
    AND (    `the home`'s `I am the sole owner`
          OR `the circumstances`'s `the charge is assessed only on the part I alone occupy as a tenant in common`
          OR `the home`'s `my only co-owner is my spouse or de facto partner`
          OR `the circumstances`'s `my co-owner lives there, may also defer, and agrees`)
    AND (NOT (`the circumstances`'s `the authority declined because the land would not cover the charges`))

§§ `ss 44(2), 45, 46, 49 -- when do deferred charges become payable?`

-- s 44(2): deferred charges "become payable and may be recovered ... if the person liable for the payment (a) dies, unless
-- section 45(1) applies; or (b) ceases to be a person entitled to the land; or (c) ceases to occupy the land as their
-- ordinary place of residence, unless (i) a person to whom section 31(1) applies continues to reside there; or (ii)
-- residence there by that person ceased by reason of ill-health, frailty or other cause not within the control of that
-- person; or (iii) [separation, the partner remaining, s 45(2) applying] ... whichever happens first."
-- s 45(1): on death, if "the land continues to be the ordinary place of residence of a surviving spouse or de facto
-- partner", liability is "further deferred until that surviving spouse or de facto partner (c) dies; or (d) ceases to be a
-- person to whom the land ... belongs, notwithstanding that the spouse or de facto partner may not be an eligible person".
-- s 45(2): on moving out, if "a spouse or de facto partner of that person continues so to occupy the land", further
-- deferred until the person dies, the partner dies or leaves, or a family court order provides otherwise.
DECLARE `What happens to me` IS ONE OF
    `I die`
    `the home stops being mine, for example I sell it`
    `I stop living there`

@export Do my deferred rates and charges become payable now (ss 44(2), 45)?
GIVEN `the event` IS A `What happens to me` @desc What has happened
      `my spouse or de facto partner goes on living there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your spouse or de facto partner continues to live there as their ordinary place of residence
      `I left because of ill-health, frailty or another cause beyond my control` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you left for a reason outside your control
      `an eligible dependant who pays the charge stays on` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a dependant within section 31(1) stays on, paying the charge
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do my deferred charges become payable` IS
    CONSIDER `the event`
    WHEN `I die` THEN NOT `my spouse or de facto partner goes on living there`
    WHEN `the home stops being mine, for example I sell it` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `I stop living there` THEN
        NOT (   `my spouse or de facto partner goes on living there`
             OR `I left because of ill-health, frailty or another cause beyond my control`
             OR `an eligible dependant who pays the charge stays on`)

-- s 46(1): deferred charges "in so far as they remain unpaid are by force of this Act a charge on the land ranking equally
-- with any other charge on that land created by an Act, and before any other kind of charge" (subject to housing debts
-- under (2)-(3), not encoded).
@export Are my unpaid deferred charges a charge on my home (s 46(1))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are my deferred charges a charge on my home` IS TRUE

-- s 49: "nothing contained in the Limitation Act 2005 prevents the administrative authority from recovering any amount"
-- deferred.
@export Can the passage of time (the Limitation Act 2005) stop my deferred charges being recovered (s 49)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can time bar recovery of my deferred charges` IS FALSE

§§ `ss 35, 38 -- changes I must report, and the penalty`

-- s 35(1): if the person "(a) ceases (i) to hold a seniors' card; or (ii) to be an eligible pensioner; or (iii) to occupy
-- the land as their ordinary place of residence; or (b) the interest ... changes ...; or (c) the land no longer belongs
-- to that person, ... the person is required forthwith to notify the administrative authority".
-- s 35(4): if "by reason only of any increase in the assets or income ... in excess of that permitted by the means test,
-- a person ceases to be an eligible person", the person "may be allowed to retain (a) the benefit of any rebate ...
-- previously ... allowed; and (b) any right to defer payment ..., until such time as a liability for payment of the full
-- amount under this Act otherwise arises, but for future charged periods must seek registration again if then eligible."
@export If I stop being eligible only because my income or assets rose above the means test, may I keep the deferment I already have (s 35(4))?
GIVEN `the only reason is that my income or assets rose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a rise in income or assets above the means test is the only reason you stopped being eligible
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I keep my existing deferment` IF `the only reason is that my income or assets rose`

-- s 38(1): an offence to "(a) ... knowingly provide information that is false or misleading ...; or (b) omit to notify
-- ... a change of circumstances, as required by section 35; or (c) ... purport ... to retain an entitlement ... or to be an
-- eligible person, knowing" otherwise. "Penalty: $1 000." (2): "A prosecution ... must be commenced within 5 years".
@export What is the most I can be fined for failing to report a change, or for a false claim (s 38(1))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined` IS 1000

@export Within how many years after the offence must a prosecution be started (s 38(2))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `Within how many years must I be prosecuted` IS 5
